@* The window's AI · MCP (SettingsWindow.axaml): the servers as cards, a program on this machine green and one reached
   over the network blue (Looks.Local), what each is in a line; Edit and Remove on the card, Add below; and, in red,
   the note that a server is enabled and named by no role (McpRoles), said where somebody can act on it. *@

<p class="label">MCP servers</p>
<p class="hint">Connect local programs through stdio or remote servers through HTTP. Enabled servers connect for each new run; running tasks keep their current settings.</p>
<p class="hint">Allow mcp__* under AI · Team to let a worker use these tools. Calls ask for approval by default. External changes are not staged or undone by Enactive.</p>
<p class="hint">A stdio server is a program the service starts, as the service's own Windows account.</p>
@if (Vm.HasMcpReachNote)
{
    <p class="hint settings-problem" role="alert">@Vm.McpReachNote</p>
}

<ul class="cards settings-cards" aria-label="MCP servers">
    @foreach (var row in Vm.McpServers)
    {
        <li class="@($"card settings-card {Looks.Local(row.IsLocal)}")">
            <span class="settings-card-text">
                <span class="card-title">@row.Name</span>
                <span class="card-meta">@row.Meta</span>
            </span>
            <span class="card-meta settings-reach">@row.Reach</span>
            <RowIcons Edit="() => row.EditCommand.Execute(null)" Remove="() => row.RemoveCommand.Execute(null)" Of="@row.Name" />
        </li>
    }
</ul>
<div class="settings-section-actions">
    <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="() => Vm.AddMcpCommand.Execute(null)">Add</button>
</div>

@code {
    // Handed down as a cascading value rather than a parameter: the view model is internal to the application layer,
    // and a component's parameters are public.
    [CascadingParameter] private SettingsViewModel Vm { get; set; } = default!;
}
